Project-local `.env` and `.env.local` files are loaded automatically if present.

Profile setup:

```sh
{{ .AuthDoc.ProfileCommand }}
```

Bind a profile to its own API base URL with `--server` when adding it, so you do
not have to repeat the flag on every call:

```sh
{{ .CommandName }} auth profile add --server https://api.staging.example.com staging
```

`{{ .CommandName }} auth setup` takes the same flag.

Each secret is prompted for without echo. In CI or automation, use `--<key>-file <path>`
(or `--<key>-file -` for stdin) to supply secrets without putting them on the command line.
Positional arguments still work but print a warning for sensitive keys.

Pick the profile every command uses, so `--profile` does not have to be repeated:

```sh
{{ .CommandName }} auth profile use staging   # `auth profile list` marks the active one
{{ .CommandName }} auth profile current       # see the active profile
{{ .CommandName }} auth profile clear         # go back to no profile
```

There is no implicit profile. Until one is named or chosen, credentials come
from the environment variables above, which is the usual shape in CI. A profile
you add is auto-selected whenever no profile is currently active — including
right after `auth profile clear`, so adding another profile silently
re-enables profiles.

Once a profile is in force it outranks the environment for its key, so
`--profile staging` cannot end up sending a key that came from the
environment — see "Server resolution order" below for how a profile's server
fits in. A profile that cannot supply a key is an error rather than a silent
fallback, and `--profile ""` turns profiles off for one command.

Server resolution order, most specific first: an explicit `--server` flag, the
profile's bound server, the `<PREFIX>_SERVER` environment variable (same prefix
as the variables above), a persisted `server set` default, then the generated
server default. `auth profile list` shows the server bound to each profile, and
`doctor` shows the one the active profile resolves to.
