## Raw Request Escape Hatch

Use the built-in raw request command when a high-level command is missing:

```sh
{{ .CommandName }} request get /path
{{ .CommandName }} request post /path < body.json
```

## Custom Commands

Bartolo keeps generated and user-owned code separate:

- CLI entrypoint: `cmd/{{ .CommandName }}/main.go`
- Generated API code: `cli/generated/`
- User-owned extensions: `cli/custom/`
- Add your own commands or hook registrations inside `cli/custom/Register(...)` so regeneration does not overwrite your work.

## Output Conventions

- Collection commands render object results as readable tables when run in an interactive terminal. The generated schema can set the default columns with `x-cli-list-fields`; otherwise the columns are inferred from the response and trimmed to fit the terminal. Use `--jmespath` for a one-off projection and `--json` for the complete response.
- Prefer `--json` when you want machine-readable output.
- Piping a response also keeps the serialized output stable for scripts and agents.
- Use `--help` on any command group or command to inspect flags and required args.
- Use `help-input` when a command accepts a request body from stdin or CLI shorthand.
- Use `server list`, `server use`, and `server set` to manage generated server defaults.
- Use `auth profile list`, `auth profile use`, `auth profile current`, and `auth profile clear` to see, pick, show, and drop the active credentials profile.
- `make build` writes the binary to `./bin/{{ .CommandName }}`.
- `make install-local` installs the binary to `~/.local/bin/{{ .CommandName }}` by default.
- `make completions` writes shell completion files into `./completions/`.
